    <comp lib="10" loc="(20,170)" name="Text">
      <a name="background" val="#f003ff4e"/>
      <a name="text" val="Map the 4-digit display to the display, of course."/>
      <a name="valign" val="base"/>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="10" loc="(20,20)" name="Text">
      <a name="background" val="#8aff954e"/>
      <a name="format" val="wrapped"/>
      <a name="text">This circuit implements a "snake" like game on the Alchitry Cu V2 fpga board with Io V2 hat. It uses the 4-digit seven segment display as a kind of simple screen, and the buttons as a D-Pad to control the direction. The snake grows each time the timer reaches zero. You can wrap around the edge of the screen (otherwise the game becomes extremely difficult.
This circuit is a bit rediculous.
For this demo, set the fpga clock speed to a few MHz, but no more than 150MHz to avoid timing constraint failures. Most anything over a few kHz should be fine though.</a>
      <a name="textwidth" val="550"/>
      <a name="valign" val="base"/>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="10" loc="(391,344)" name="Text">
      <a name="background" val="#f003ff4e"/>
      <a name="text">Map the W/A/S/D buttons to
Up/Left/Down/Right buttons.
Map R to the side button.</a>
      <a name="valign" val="base"/>
    </comp>
